WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:09.840
One fine day, after returning home from lectures,
Zara developed extremely high

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.040
fever.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:17.180
Also, she had frequent severe chills and sweating
intermediately!

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:18.980
What was really wrong with her?

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:24.510
A few diagnostic tests revealed that Zara
was suffering from Malaria!

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:25.510
Yes!

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:28.509
And
now, she was given all the possible best treatments

00:00:28.509 --> 00:00:32.469
to recover as quickly as
possible!

00:00:32.469 --> 00:00:37.040
But how did she get infected with malaria
in the first place?

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:38.760
What could have
caused it?

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:42.499
And how did the infectious agent take over
her body?

00:00:42.499 --> 00:00:47.260
Let us learn this infectious disease in detail
in this video!

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:51.320
To begin with, can you tell me, what causes
malaria?

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.820
We have always heard….

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:56.679
Mosquito bites are the root cause of malaria.

00:00:56.679 --> 00:00:59.489
So are mosquitoes responsible for
this infection?

00:00:59.489 --> 00:01:00.489
Nope!

00:01:00.489 --> 00:01:04.740
You will be surprised to know that mosquitoes
do not cause

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:08.150
malaria, but just spread it.

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:10.690
And what do we mean by this?

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:15.030
The actual causative agent of Malaria is the
protozoan called Plasmodium.

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:22.840
Plasmodium vivax, Plasmodium malaria, Plasmodium
falciparum are a few species

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:26.790
of Plasmodium that cause malaria in humans.

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:31.470
Among these, Plasmodium
falciparum is the most violent, and can also

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:32.470
be life-threatening.

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:36.740
So from where
does a mosquito come into the picture?

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:44.310
Well, the protozoan Plasmodium lives as
a parasite in the female Anopheles mosquito.

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:48.540
From there, it gets transmitted to
humans with a mosquito bite.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:53.340
Hence, it is the infected mosquito BITE which
can cause malaria, and not the

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:54.340
insect itself!

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:55.610
Plasmodium (parasite) → Female Anopheles
mosquito → Transferred → Humans

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:58.500
→ mosquito bite
Thus, the complete life cycle of the malarial

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:02.840
parasitic protozoa involves two hosts.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:07.430
First one is the female Anopheles mosquito
and the second one is the human

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:09.170
host.

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:14.030
Let us understand the complete life cycle
of the malarial parasite.

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:18.450
It all begins when an infected mosquito bites
a human.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:22.999
This helps transfer the
malarial parasite in the human body.

00:02:22.999 --> 00:02:27.939
In order to enjoy her blood meal, the female
Anopheles mosquito secretes her

00:02:27.939 --> 00:02:33.069
saliva into the human bloodstream to prevent
blood coagulation.

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:35.980
It is with this
secretion, that the parasite gets a chance

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:38.659
to enter the human body.

00:02:38.659 --> 00:02:43.540
The disease causing Plasmodium, gets transferred
from mosquitoes to humans in

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:46.549
the form of sporozoites.

00:02:46.549 --> 00:02:51.090
Sporozoites is a stage in the life cycle of
Plasmodium.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:55.989
At this particular stage they
are capable to cause an infection in the human

00:02:55.989 --> 00:02:57.599
body.

00:02:57.599 --> 00:02:59.890
And how do they do that?

00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:03.590
Once they enter the bloodstream of the humans
they eventually reach their

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:06.069
target, that is the liver.

00:03:06.069 --> 00:03:09.909
Here, they attack the liver cells and stay
inside them for

00:03:09.909 --> 00:03:11.370
quite a long time.

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:15.959
Here, they reproduce asexually, in order to
increase their

00:03:15.959 --> 00:03:16.959
number.

00:03:16.959 --> 00:03:19.920
After that, they release themselves back into
the bloodstream by

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.090
bursting out the liver cells.

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:24.260
The form in which they are released into the

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:28.190
bloodstream is known as merozoites.

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:33.340
These merozoites now target the Red Blood
Cells, the RBCs.

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:38.220
Now in the RBCs, the merozoites develop into
a ring like structure called

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:40.510
Trophozoite.

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:43.389
This form can have two fates.

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:50.409
Most of the trophozoites begin with
asexual cycle to give rise to new merozoites.

00:03:50.409 --> 00:03:53.180
And this occurs exponentially.

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:56.069
The
number of merozoites produced is too large

00:03:56.069 --> 00:03:58.079
for the RBCs to contain.

00:03:58.079 --> 00:04:01.029
Hence, they
are released out, with the bursting of the

00:04:01.029 --> 00:04:02.689
RBCs.

00:04:02.689 --> 00:04:07.659
The released merozoites can now
attack new RBCs and keep increasing their

00:04:07.659 --> 00:04:10.599
population exponentially.

00:04:10.599 --> 00:04:13.959
The other
fate of the trophozoites is entering a sexual

00:04:13.959 --> 00:04:15.870
cycle.

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:20.110
With this, the trophozoites give
rise to two different gametocytes.

00:04:20.110 --> 00:04:26.160
Gametocytes in simple terms are like the germ
cells of plasmodium.

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:28.819
When a non-infected mosquito approaches the
infected

00:04:28.819 --> 00:04:33.700
human for a blood meal, then these gametocytes
quickly pass into the mosquito’s

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:36.539
body with the sucked blood.

00:04:36.539 --> 00:04:42.160
Now here, in the mosquito’s body, both,
male and female gametocytes, which are

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:46.160
like the germ cells, fuse to form a zygote.

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:51.870
That is, the fertilization of these
gametocytes results in the formation and development

00:04:51.870 --> 00:05:13.169
of zygote

00:05:13.169 --> 00:05:34.740
in

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:45.310
the
mosquito’s gut.

